/ref 17592186382648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176089 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1045 "The Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176160 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fb65b447-b251-5b8b-84fb-25df81ffc211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477614 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477615 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477616 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=1$,  where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n- **II.** $\\text{Im}(T)$ contains no vectors of ${R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4acbb26f-7e4b-53ce-a37d-bd74763516c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176476 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The image of $T$ contains the vector zero vector $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186176932 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176932 #edb/ref 1061 70373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79dd6a5b-b38e-5027-95bc-e8b46a7f3a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176933 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176933 #edb/ref 1061 70374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9da32ac-2fba-5b76-a770-d303f55e3aff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176934 #edb/ref 1061 70377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad2e7f46-d633-5065-9a91-a94b5e3bcf75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176935 #edb/ref 1061 70380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51ed8d83-4d25-56a7-994d-262c6c456b9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the following linear transformation: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, it can only be one of the following:  \n\n- the whole of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$\n\n- a line through the origin, or\n\n- a single point (vector), namely the origin $(0,0).$\n\nLet's denote the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ by $T.$ It turns out that the dimension of the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ depends on $\\text{rank}(T)$ (or equivalently on $\\text{nullity}(T)$ by the rank-nullity theorem). Indeed, we have the following:\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=2,$ then the two columns of $T$ are linearly independent. This means $T$ is invertible and therefore, $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) =  \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto  $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7d/7de193042587cb9362641cbb541477a5e4951e96d9e82f074fb0cc3a86a80d3a.svg)\n\n![](images/88/88baa47cae14d4a43d59b366c13b3c29376678edef2fe34ff505a942ef6af986.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=1,$ then there is a nonzero vector that generates the image of $\\mathbf{T}.$ In this case, the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a one-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^2$. So, it is a line passing through the origin.\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=2x\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/49/4952615b0c4ddf85cc6d60483f8c0587093c67734a320cde913644fe8fdafb55.svg)\nNotice that the points $(2,4)$ and $(1,2),$ defined by the columns of $T,$ lie on the line $y=2x.$\n\n![](images/03/0322f183c16fe82515277d10cf27a93ae753f8eadf7eeb1db4d3c5e404480b09.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=0$ then,  according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that  $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})=\\mathbf{0}$ for all $\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Bbb R^2$. So, in this case, we have that\n![](images/4a/4a0ce67cffc02e28edffb9efeedca2e21ffc7c0d8b72ccfa0566cb29f6679865.svg)\nwhich maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\{(0,0)\\},$ i.e., the set containing only the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/12/1252907a1e9767201dd326945f328f91fc493ea283b244dbfee42a7c4248d9e6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1065 8344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0ec0551e-64f7-52a7-a0e7-827e6a8944f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470122 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470123 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470124 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366052 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366052 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366052 #edb/ref 1071 "If the linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-3x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨1,3⟩$ belongs to the range of $T$\n\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n\n- **III.** $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34581764-4087-5b06-9eff-4679fe703405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366052 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. \nNotice that the point $(1,3)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=-3x$ since $3\\ne -3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,3⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \nsince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. Hence, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. \nFor example,  the point $(1,-3)$ satisfies the equation  $y=-3x$ since $-3=-3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,-3⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$ Thus, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {0}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366752 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366752 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366752 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366752 #edb/ref 1071 "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366752 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1afcec6c-a31f-5b1b-826d-d0232bfbe82b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366752 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,-1)$ and $(-1,1),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1-(-1)}{-1-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 