 linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366759 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eff617ee-6ce3-51e8-bcd8-61c31ebef8f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366759 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,4)$ and $(2,8),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8-4}{2-1}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=4x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1ca72594-5be6-5643-b9f7-7c9ff9fc7016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366761 #edb/ref 1080 "{R}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "98ef481a-705d-5f82-9a19-92ceb01cfee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366762 #edb/ref 1080 "(0,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366762 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366762 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a54ef0a-64c9-5842-8675-a775bf4e73ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366763 #edb/ref 1080 "y=4x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366763 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366763 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8f8f571-4006-5417-916b-8b1ccb869c3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366764 #edb/ref 1080 "y=2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366764 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366764 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52efde24-9a00-5780-9f50-bdae742b41f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366765 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-4x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366765 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366765 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fccc2756-9459-5499-8770-f77a30708a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366780 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453009 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366780 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453010 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366780 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453011 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366780 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366780 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366780 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨2,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366780 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2335439b-17b4-5a3d-b1ce-91063205bf0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366780 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1,\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $(2,1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=2x$ since $1\\ne 2\\cdot 2.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨2,1⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186429129 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429129 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3645->1049. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429129 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048742 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429130 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429130 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen